    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.icons\n\n### Description\n\nConsider the following scenario:\n- `mini.nvim` is installed, but the user don't want to enable `mini.icons`\n- `nvim-web-devicons` is installed and enabled\n\nIn the above scenario, the intended icon usage is to use `nvim-web-devicons` for all plugins that require icons. However, `mini.icons` can still be accessed by calling the module directly, even though its setup is not called. Example:  `require(\"mini.icons\").get(...)`. Hence, plugins which support both `mini.icons` and `nvim-web-devicons` might unintentionally use `mini.icons` instead. This is the behaviour of `snacks.nvim` as can be seen from this [snippet](https://github.com/folke/snacks.nvim/blob/bc0630e43be5699bb94dadc302c0d21615421d93/lua/snacks/util/init.lua#L125-L154) from its codebase, where it checks for `mini.icon` first before checking for `nvim-web-devicons`.\n\nI saw the documentation of `mini.nvim-disabling-recipes`, and thought maybe we could use that approach to toggle `mini.icons`, i.e. use `{g,b}:miniicons_disable`. Alternatively, maybe it can be disabled by default and only be enabled if the setup method of `mini.icons` is called.\n\n",
    "closed_at": "2025-09-06T06:00:22Z",
